### Importing from a File
<hr style="width:100%;border-bottom:3px solid black;">

Instead of an inline `define`, a module can be an existing file:

```Makefile
$(call mk.import.module, file=path/to/module.mk namespace=mymod)
```

`def=` and `file=` are mutually exclusive (exactly one is required).  With `file=` and no `namespace=`, the module is named after the file's basename.

### Partial Imports
<hr style="width:100%;border-bottom:3px solid black;">

By default the whole module is imported.  To bring in only part of one, add a `targets=` or `defs=` selector (mutually exclusive); each is a name or a glob (`*`/`?`):

```Makefile
# only the targets matching the glob (still namespaced + compiled)
$(call mk.import.module, def=MyModule targets='build.*')

# only the named define blocks
$(call mk.import.module, file=lib.mk defs='helper_*')
```

The selector runs as the first stage of the staging pipeline (`select -> namespace -> compile`), so the chosen members are namespaced and compiled exactly as in a full import -- the rest are simply never brought into scope.  Selection is **by name only**: a selected target whose prerequisite is an unselected sibling is the caller's responsibility.

### The Declaration Family
<hr style="width:100%;border-bottom:3px solid black;">

`mk.import.module` is one member of a broader **declaration** namespace -- parse-time verbs that code-generate make state before any recipe runs.  `declare.module` is the module entry; its siblings bring other kinds of things (single defines, targets, plugins, compose files, polyglots) into scope by the same convention.

